            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Three Signals in This Week's Material Pool Changes</h2>
<p>As we enter Q3, the difficulty of cold launching new products on TikTok Shop has visibly increased. Last week, a home goods client we serve reported that the consumption speed for new products slowed by nearly half compared to three months ago with the same budget. This is not an isolated case—multiple category operators this week mentioned that the cold launch cycle extended from one week to two weeks or even longer.</p>
<p>This pressure stems from the growing richness of the platform's material library and increasing user thresholds for repetitive content. We've identified three signals worth noting this week.</p>
<h3>Signal 1: UGC Content Click-Through Rates Rebounding</h3>
<p>The conversion efficiency of pure product showcase videos has diverged this week. Average click-through rates for pure sales pitch style content dropped about 15%, while UGC-style content with authentic user scenarios saw click-through rates rebound, exceeding 3.8% in some categories.</p>
<p>Two factors explain this: First, the platform's recent traffic preference for "native-feeling" content; second, accumulated user fatigue with hard sell tactics. For new product cold launches, this means material types need to shift toward UGC direction earlier, rather than relying on traditional product showcase logic.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Practical reference</strong>: During the first week of cold launch, UGC-style content testing ratio should be no less than 40%.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Signal 2: First-Three-Seconds Completion Rate Becomes the First Gate</h3>
<p>Backend data this week universally shows that materials failing to capture attention in the first 3 seconds struggle with subsequent consumption. Many operators have begun adopting "quick pain point entry + subtitle highlighting" editing patterns—starting directly with usage scenarios or problem-solution presentations.</p>
<p>This approach does show improvement. We observed a utility app account that, after adopting the "open with 2 seconds directly addressing pain points + background music beat sync" template, saw average completion rates rise from 28% to 41%, with corresponding consumption growth.</p>
<h3>Signal 3: Material Reuse Strategies Becoming More Refined</h3>
<p>During cold launch, many teams' approach is to batch-produce materials for rapid testing, eliminating underperformers. But this week we noticed a change: top teams have started "material deconstruction and reuse"—breaking one high-performing material into multiple short versions, testing different segments for conversion efficiency.</p>
<p>The benefit is maximizing already-validated user attention elements. For new product cold launches, if you can generate one data-acceptable material, quickly scaling through deconstruction and reuse is more efficient than re-batched testing.</p>
<h2>Cold Launch Material Pool Building Steps</h2>
<p>Based on the above changes, we recommend building the cold launch material pool as follows:</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Define Material Type Distribution</strong><br />
- UGC-style content: 40%, focused on authentic usage scenarios<br />
- Product feature showcase: 30%, duration 15-30 seconds<br />
- Pain point solution type: 30%, opening directly addresses problems</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Set Testing Budget Allocation</strong><br />
- Individual material testing budget should be no less than $50-100<br />
- Prioritize testing UGC-style and pain point categories<br />
- Maintain at least 3 materials per type for A/B comparison</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Establish Material Elimination Mechanism</strong><br />
- Pause materials underperforming within 48 hours<br />
- Do not add budget to materials with click-through rates below 2%<br />
- Preserve original project files of high-performing materials for later deconstruction and reuse</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Deconstruct and Scale</strong><br />
- Identify core elements of high-performing materials (hooks, visuals, music rhythm)<br />
- Generate 3-5 derivative versions<br />
- Test derivative versions in new ad groups to avoid internal competition</p>
<h2>Weekly Summary</h2>
<p>The core challenge during cold launch is fundamentally "how to quickly obtain data validation amid material competition." This week's changes tell us that UGC styling and strong hook thinking are regaining prominence, while traffic costs for pure product displays are rising.</p>
<p>We recommend that operators introduce UGC concepts earlier in next week's material planning, and prepare deconstruction solutions for high-performing materials in advance. If consumption speed remains sluggish, prioritize checking first-three-seconds completion rates rather than blindly adding budget.</p>
<p>Next week, we'll continue observing trends in material lifecycle and advertising cost changes.</p>
